  "rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, the passage of about 250 words that came immediately BEFORE the current text in its story.",
  "text": "The morning had begun with an unusual stillness, a heavy, oppressive quiet that settled over the coastal village of Oakhaven like a damp wool blanket. Elias, the lighthouse keeper, had risen before dawn, his joints protesting the cold dampness that seeped through the stone walls of his quarters. He had dressed in his heavy wool coat, the fabric stiff with age, and checked the oil reserves in the lamp room, ensuring the wicks were trimmed and the reservoirs full. The routine was a comfort, a series of small, manageable tasks that kept the chaos of the world at bay. As he descended the spiral staircase, the iron steps groaned under his weight, a familiar sound that echoed in the hollow tower. He paused at the gallery door, peering out at the sea. The water was unnaturally calm, a mirror reflecting the pale, washed-out sky. There were no waves, no whitecaps, just a flat, grey expanse that stretched to the horizon. It was a sight that should have been peaceful, yet it filled Elias with a deep, primal unease. He had seen this before, decades ago, during the Great Storm of '98. The sea had been just as still, just as deceptive, before it erupted in a fury that had shattered the harbor wall and claimed three fishing boats. He had been young then, eager and confident, but he had learned his lesson. He had spent the early hours reinforcing the shutters, securing the loose stones on the roof, and checking the emergency generator. The village below was waking up, the first lights flickering on in the cottages, but there was no activity on the docks. The fishermen, usually out by sunrise, had stayed in, their faces grim as they watched the sky. Elias could see them from the gallery, a cluster of dark figures huddled together, pointing at the horizon. He felt a chill that had nothing to do with the temperature. The air was thick, charged with a static electricity that made the hair on his arms stand on end. He knew, with a certainty that bypassed logic, that something