  "text": "The motel room smelled of stale cigarettes and industrial cleaner, a scent that clung to the back of Elias’s throat like a bad taste. He sat on the edge of the sagging mattress, the springs groaning under his weight, staring at the ceiling where a water stain had spread into the shape of a jagged map. Five years. That was how long it had been since he last stood on that cliffside, since the last time he had seen Clara’s face illuminated by the lighthouse beam. The silence in the room was heavy, pressing against his eardrums, broken only by the distant hum of the highway and the occasional crash of waves against the rocks below. He reached into his jacket pocket and pulled out the letter again. The paper was soft from handling, the edges frayed, but the words remained stark and unyielding. *The sea remembers.* It was a phrase they had used when they were young, when the world was still a place of possibility rather than consequence. Now, it felt like a threat. He had tried to forget, to build a life in the city where the air was dry and the horizon was a flat line of concrete and steel. But the memories had a way of seeping through the cracks, like water through a dam. He thought of the night they had made their promise, the wind whipping their hair, the stars burning cold and bright above them. He had broken that promise. He had run. And now, the sea was calling him back, not with a gentle whisper, but with a roar. He stood up, his legs feeling unsteady, and walked to the window. The rain was beginning to fall, a fine mist that blurred the view of the dark ocean. He knew he had to go. He knew he had to face what he had left behind. He grabbed his keys, his heart pounding in his chest like a trapped bird, and stepped out into the night, the cold air hitting his face like a slap."
