{
  "rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, the passage of about 250 words that came immediately BEFORE the current text in its story.",
  "text": "The envelope had arrived three days ago, slipped under the door of his apartment in a city that felt increasingly like a prison. No return address, just his name written in Clara’s distinctive, looping script. Elias had stared at it for hours, the paper trembling in his hands, before finally tearing it open. Inside was a single sheet of parchment, yellowed with age, and a small, smooth stone from the beach where they had spent their summers. He had kept the stone in his pocket ever since, a talisman against the growing dread that had settled in his stomach. The city had been loud, chaotic, and indifferent, a perfect place to bury a past that refused to stay buried. He had told himself that leaving was the only way to survive, that the cliffside, the lighthouse, and the promise they had made under the stars were part of a life he had outgrown. But the silence of the motel room, the smell of the ocean air that seemed to seep through the cracked window, told a different story. He remembered the weight of her hand in his, the way her eyes had reflected the lighthouse beam, a beacon of hope that he had foolishly ignored. He had run because he was afraid, afraid of the depth of his feelings, afraid of the consequences of staying. Now, standing in the doorway of his life, he realized that running had only made the distance between them wider, not smaller. The letter was not an invitation; it was a summons. The sea was not waiting for him to be ready; it was waiting for him to arrive. He looked at the stone in his palm, feeling its cool surface against his skin, and knew that there was no more time to hesitate. The past was not a place he could visit; it was a debt he had to pay. He closed his eyes, took a deep breath, and prepared to step back into the storm."
