{
  "rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, the passage of about 250 words that came immediately BEFORE the current text in its story.",
  "text": "The argument had started over something trivial, a misplaced set of keys or a forgotten dinner reservation, but it had spiraled into a chasm of unspoken resentments that neither of them could bridge. Clara stood by the kitchen island, her arms crossed tightly over her chest, her face pale and drawn. Elias felt the heat rising in his neck, the familiar, suffocating pressure of being misunderstood, of being the villain in a story he hadn’t written. He wanted to scream, to explain the long hours at the firm, the sleepless nights spent worrying about their future, but the words stuck in his throat, choked by the sheer exhaustion of maintaining a facade of strength. He looked at the life they had built together—the framed photos on the mantel, the half-read books on the coffee table, the quiet comfort of their shared routine—and felt a sudden, violent urge to shatter it all. It wasn’t that he didn’t love her; it was that he didn’t recognize himself anymore. He was drowning in the expectations of others, in the weight of a career that consumed him, in the silence that had grown between them like ivy strangling a tree. He grabbed his coat from the hook by the door, the fabric heavy and cold against his skin. Clara didn’t stop him. She didn’t call out or plead. She just watched him, her eyes wide and hollow, reflecting a grief that was already setting in. He paused at the threshold, looking back one last time, hoping for a sign, a gesture, anything that would anchor him to this place. But there was only the hum of the refrigerator and the distant sound of traffic. He stepped out into the night, the door clicking shut behind him with a finality that echoed in his bones. The air was cold, biting at his exposed skin, and for the first time in years, he felt truly, terrifyingly alone."
