  "text": "The *Vanguard* had docked with a shuddering groan, the magnetic clamps locking into place with a finality that felt less like arrival and more like capture. Elias stood at the airlock, watching the derelict station loom against the starfield, its hull scarred by micrometeoroids and the silent passage of centuries. It was a ghost ship, a monument to a failure that the Committee had long since buried in classified archives. Commander Vane adjusted her uniform, her eyes scanning the darkened corridors of the docking bay with the predatory focus of a hawk. \"Stay close,\" she ordered, her voice low and clipped. \"The sensors are picking up residual energy signatures, but nothing active. If this is a trap, it’s a very old one.\" Elias nodded, though his stomach churned with a dread he couldn’t quite name. He had spent weeks studying the station’s history, piecing together fragments of logs that spoke of a project called 'Project Aether,' a theoretical attempt to harness the background radiation of the universe. The logs ended abruptly, mid-sentence, as if the writers had been erased from existence. As they stepped into the main corridor, the emergency lights flickered on, casting long, distorted shadows that seemed to stretch and warp in ways that defied geometry. The air was cold, biting at their exposed skin, and the silence was so profound it pressed against their eardrums. Vane moved with practiced efficiency, her weapon raised, while Elias trailed behind, his hand resting instinctively on the data pad in his pocket. He felt a strange pull, a magnetic tug in the center of his chest, drawing him deeper into the station’s heart. It was subtle, almost imperceptible, but it was there, a whisper in the static of his thoughts. He looked back at Vane, who was already moving forward, her face a mask of professional detachment. He wanted to tell her, to warn her that the station was watching, that it was waiting. But the words stuck in his throat, choked by the overwhelming sense of inevitability that hung in the air like smoke."
}

{
  "rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, the passage of about 250 words that came immediately BEFORE the current text in its story.",
  "text": "The *Vanguard*’s engines had cut to a silent idle hours ago, leaving them drifting in the void of the Oort Cloud’s outer fringe. Elias sat in the cramped observation deck, his forehead pressed against the cold glass, watching the stars smear into streaks of light as they decelerated. The mission briefing had been sparse, almost suspiciously so. Commander Vane had handed him a single data chip and a list of coordinates, her expression unreadable. "Project Aether," she had said, the name tasting like ash in her mouth. "It’s been dead for fifty years. We’re just here to confirm the site is inert and secure any remaining data cores. Nothing more." But Elias knew better. He had seen the classified files, the ones that didn’t officially exist. He had seen the final transmission from the station, a burst of static that, when decoded, revealed a single, repeating phrase: *It is awake.*

The crew was minimal, a skeleton staff of specialists who had been pulled from various deep-space assignments with little explanation. There was a tension in the air, a collective holding of breath that made the silence feel heavy. Elias tried to focus on the star charts, but his mind kept drifting back to the logs he had studied in secret. The scientists on the station hadn’t just been experimenting with background radiation; they had been trying to tap into the fabric of spacetime itself. They had succeeded, or so it seemed, until the day the logs stopped. The last entry was from Dr. Aris Thorne, the project lead. *We opened the door. Now we cannot close it. The energy is not coming from outside. It is coming from within. It is looking back.*

A chime sounded, breaking his reverie. The docking bay was ready. Elias stood up, his legs feeling unsteady. He looked at his reflection in the glass, seeing a man who looked older than his years, haunted by knowledge he couldn’t share. He adjusted his collar, trying to steady his breathing. This was it. The moment of truth. He walked toward the airlock, the magnetic hum of the ship growing louder in his ears, a prelude to the unknown that waited in the dark."
}

